---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL, SITE_NAMES } from '../../data/site-config';
import { srcLink } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { displayDate } from '../../lib/format';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Indonesia calendar 2027 publisher: who we are, sources';
const description = 'Who publishes this Indonesia 2027 holiday calendar: Radif Partners, independent, selling no travel. Where holidays, collective leave and Hijri dates come from.';
const s = (k: string, t: string) => srcLink(k, lang, t);
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>About {SITE_NAMES[lang]}</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>Every September the Indonesian government publishes a joint decree of three ministers listing next year's national holidays and collective leave days (cuti bersama). What then circulates is mostly an image of the table on social media, an unsourced PDF, or an article that computes nothing. This site reads the decree itself, lays it out as a calendar and works out what people actually need: when the long weekends are, how many leave days to take, how many working days a month has, and how many days remain until Lebaran.</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The publisher is {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds tools from public rules and data. {AUTHOR_NAME} is not a government body and uses no state emblem or ministry logo. The site sells no tickets, tours or calendars and carries no affiliate links.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
    <p>National holidays and collective leave come from the annexes of the <Fragment set:html={s('skb-2027', '2027 joint decree')} /> and the <Fragment set:html={s('skb-2026', '2026 joint decree')} /> published by the Coordinating Ministry for Human Development and Culture. The decrees refer to <Fragment set:html={s('keppres-8-2024', 'Presidential Decree 8 of 2024')} /> on public holidays. Annual leave follows Article 79 of the Manpower Act as amended by <Fragment set:html={s('uu-6-2023', 'Law 6 of 2023')} />. Hijri dates outside those fixed by the decree use the <Fragment set:html={s('ummulqura', 'Umm al-Qura')} /> calendar, and the new moon is computed with a formula we check against <Fragment set:html={s('nasa-fase-bulan', 'NASA tables')} />. How it all works is on the <a href={route('method', lang)}>method</a> page.</p>
    <h2>Your data</h2>
    <p>Every tool runs in your browser. The year, leave days or date of birth you enter are neither sent nor stored. There is no account and no form. Details are in the <a href={route('privacy', lang)}>privacy notice</a>.</p>
    <h2>What the site does not do</h2>
    <p>It sets no dates: that is the government's job, and for the start of Ramadan, Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha the Minister of Religious Affairs decides after the isbat session. It does not list school holidays or regional holidays, which provinces and schools set themselves.</p>
    <h2>Write to us</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> if a date looks wrong. Decrees last checked on {displayDate(K.TGL_BACA, 'en-GB')}.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
